WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:04.201
Welcome to Module 4 of the
HR-Driven Identity learning path:

2
00:00:04.201 --> 00:00:08.437
assign access from attributes,
and review what a move leaves.

3
00:00:08.437 --> 00:00:14.376
This is Gate 4, Access and movers, with
Chapter 04 of the developer workbook.

4
00:00:14.376 --> 00:00:19.013
Module 3 got a new hire ready on
day one, with an onboarding package.

5
00:00:19.013 --> 00:00:21.487
Department access comes from this gate.

6
00:00:21.487 --> 00:00:26.214
In this module you'll lint access package
assignment policies before they reach the

7
00:00:26.214 --> 00:00:29.590
tenant, scan the tenant's policies
for the member-of operator,

8
00:00:29.590 --> 00:00:33.438
and list what a move leaves behind
for the manager of record to review.

9
00:00:33.438 --> 00:00:38.538
The code runs on your laptop, against a
fictional population and a fictional mover.

10
00:00:38.538 --> 00:00:44.220
It's illustrative: unit-tested against
fixtures, not run against a live tenant.

11
00:00:47.970 --> 00:00:49.607
Why does this gate need checks?

12
00:00:49.607 --> 00:00:53.958
When HR moves a person to a new
organization, a rule on the organization

13
00:00:53.958 --> 00:00:58.233
code should take away the old
department's access and give the new one.

14
00:00:58.233 --> 00:01:01.920
Automatic assignment policies in
entitlement management do that.

15
00:01:01.920 --> 00:01:05.020
But a rule can be wrong in ways
the portal won't tell you.

16
00:01:05.020 --> 00:01:08.770
Two policies on one package
can both match the same user.

17
00:01:08.770 --> 00:01:10.670
A rule can read a display name.

18
00:01:10.670 --> 00:01:13.630
Or a rule can use the member-of operator,

19
00:01:13.630 --> 00:01:17.145
which Learn quarantines
from November 3, 2026.

20
00:01:17.145 --> 00:01:20.645
And no rule can see access
the person was given by hand.

21
00:01:20.645 --> 00:01:25.440
So in this module, the lint catches the
first three before a policy reaches the

22
00:01:25.440 --> 00:01:29.336
tenant, a scan finds member-of in
the tenant's existing policies,

23
00:01:29.336 --> 00:01:33.150
and the residual review lists
what a move leaves behind.

24
00:01:36.900 --> 00:01:39.711
Here's what you'll be able to
do by the end of this module,

25
00:01:39.711 --> 00:01:42.075
which takes about fifty
minutes with the lab.

26
00:01:42.075 --> 00:01:46.549
You'll explain why each access
package gets one automatic policy,

27
00:01:46.549 --> 00:01:48.538
and why no rule uses member-of.

28
00:01:48.538 --> 00:01:51.984
You'll run the policy
lint over a population,

29
00:01:51.984 --> 00:01:56.388
and explain three of its rules:
A A oh one, oh two and oh nine.

30
00:01:56.388 --> 00:02:00.488
You'll run the member-of scan on an
export of the tenant's policies.

31
00:02:00.488 --> 00:02:03.126
And you'll list a mover's residual access,

32
00:02:03.126 --> 00:02:07.125
classify each item, and read
the transfer-to-review interval.

33
00:02:07.125 --> 00:02:08.863
Two modules come first.

34
00:02:08.863 --> 00:02:14.088
From Module 1: a move reaches the directory
through the pipeline, never by hand.

35
00:02:14.088 --> 00:02:18.348
From Module 3: the day-one workflow
requests the onboarding package,

36
00:02:18.348 --> 00:02:21.087
and department access
comes from this gate.

37
00:02:21.087 --> 00:02:24.361
Lab 2 needs a worldwide
or G C C test tenant

38
00:02:24.361 --> 00:02:28.580
with Entra ID Governance
trial or test licenses.

39
00:02:32.330 --> 00:02:33.943
Read the guide's Step 4.

40
00:02:33.943 --> 00:02:36.081
It sets six rules for this gate.

41
00:02:36.081 --> 00:02:38.305
One automatic policy per package.

42
00:02:38.305 --> 00:02:39.343
No member-of.

43
00:02:39.343 --> 00:02:44.042
A grace period for movers, so a move
doesn't cut someone off mid-handover.

44
00:02:44.042 --> 00:02:46.892
Manager approval, with
a fallback approver.

45
00:02:46.892 --> 00:02:49.293
The mover workflow's residual review.

46
00:02:49.293 --> 00:02:52.354
And privileged access kept
out of rules altogether.

47
00:02:52.354 --> 00:02:53.567
Why no member-of?

48
00:02:53.567 --> 00:02:56.858
From November 3, 2026,
Learn quarantines an

49
00:02:56.858 --> 00:03:00.618
automatic assignment
policy whose rule uses it.

50
00:03:00.618 --> 00:03:05.555
The policy stays, but it adds and removes
nothing until the operator is removed,

51
00:03:05.555 --> 00:03:09.429
and Learn publishes a read-only
script to find those policies.

52
00:03:09.429 --> 00:03:14.841
Then read the design in Chapter 10 of the
Federal Modernization Workbook's HR-Driven

53
00:03:14.841 --> 00:03:19.248
Identity book: its rule patterns,
access packages and access reviews,

54
00:03:19.248 --> 00:03:21.567
and where dynamic membership stops.

55
00:03:21.567 --> 00:03:25.000
Its Chapter 08 says
what a move must start.

56
00:03:27.930 --> 00:03:32.880
Here are the program's policy files, kept
as reviewed version one point oh bodies.

57
00:03:32.880 --> 00:03:35.967
Two are automatic
policies, one per package.

58
00:03:35.967 --> 00:03:40.778
Program Operations matches organization
code O R G one hundred, and

59
00:03:40.778 --> 00:03:46.618
Infrastructure Services matches O R G two
ten, each for employees and contractors.

60
00:03:46.618 --> 00:03:50.925
Each one both creates assignments
when a user enters scope and removes

61
00:03:50.925 --> 00:03:55.306
them when the user leaves it, with a
seven-day grace period before removal.

62
00:03:55.306 --> 00:03:59.805
The third is a request policy for
infrastructure administrator eligibility.

63
00:03:59.805 --> 00:04:02.573
It's scoped to employees in O R G two ten,

64
00:04:02.573 --> 00:04:05.967
but it doesn't assign
anything automatically.

65
00:04:05.967 --> 00:04:08.779
It only decides who may
request eligibility.

66
00:04:08.779 --> 00:04:11.430
It's marked tier T one, privileged.

67
00:04:11.430 --> 00:04:14.915
Notice that every rule is
written on the codes HR owns,

68
00:04:14.915 --> 00:04:19.780
the organization code and the worker
type, never on a department name.

69
00:04:23.530 --> 00:04:26.836
The lint has nine rules,
and it checks each rule over

70
00:04:26.836 --> 00:04:30.142
a population of users, which
is how it finds overlap.

71
00:04:30.142 --> 00:04:32.729
A A oh one is a rule on member-of.

72
00:04:32.729 --> 00:04:35.634
A A oh two is users
who match more than one

73
00:04:35.634 --> 00:04:39.505
automatic policy on one
package; they can lose access.

74
00:04:39.505 --> 00:04:44.718
A A oh three is more identities in scope
than Learn's fifteen thousand per policy.

75
00:04:44.718 --> 00:04:49.181
A A oh four warns on a rule that
reads no coded slot or worker type.

76
00:04:49.181 --> 00:04:53.992
A A oh five is an automatic policy
that doesn't both create and remove.

77
00:04:53.992 --> 00:04:56.634
A A oh six warns on a
grace period over the

78
00:04:56.634 --> 00:04:59.917
program's fourteen days,
which is design guidance.

79
00:04:59.917 --> 00:05:04.893
A A oh seven is a request policy without
the manager and a fallback approver.

80
00:05:04.893 --> 00:05:09.768
A A oh eight sends a rule the evaluator
can't read to the dynamic-rules kit.

81
00:05:09.768 --> 00:05:13.960
And A A oh nine is tier one
access assigned by rule.

82
00:05:17.710 --> 00:05:22.085
The residual review classifies
everything a mover holds after the move.

83
00:05:22.085 --> 00:05:27.260
Take the lab's mover, from O R
G one hundred to O R G two ten.

84
00:05:27.260 --> 00:05:29.985
The new department's
baseline is kept by rule.

85
00:05:29.985 --> 00:05:33.459
The old department's
baseline is removed by rule

86
00:05:33.459 --> 00:05:36.760
after the seven-day grace
period, by October 26.

87
00:05:36.760 --> 00:05:39.909
Groups that sit behind a
package are package-managed,

88
00:05:39.909 --> 00:05:41.985
and a dynamic group is rule-managed.

89
00:05:41.985 --> 00:05:44.097
None of those need a person to decide.

90
00:05:44.097 --> 00:05:45.348
Two classes do.

91
00:05:45.348 --> 00:05:50.786
Access the mover requested, here a grants
reporting workspace, goes to review.

92
00:05:50.786 --> 00:05:56.460
And groups held outside packages, by direct
or nested assignment, go to review too.

93
00:05:56.460 --> 00:06:00.772
Those are the items a rule can't see,
and they go to the manager of record.

94
00:06:00.772 --> 00:06:05.147
The time from the transfer to a
finished review is P S five's metric,

95
00:06:05.147 --> 00:06:09.560
and R V oh two flags a review
still open ten days after the move.

96
00:06:13.310 --> 00:06:14.035
Try it.

97
00:06:14.035 --> 00:06:19.597
Lab 1 runs in the hr-lifecycle folder,
and nothing signs in or sends a request.

98
00:06:19.597 --> 00:06:22.861
Step one lists the reads,
and their permissions.

99
00:06:22.861 --> 00:06:27.280
Three GET calls: the assignment policies
and the assignments in entitlement

100
00:06:27.280 --> 00:06:30.940
management, which need
EntitlementManagement dot Read dot All,

101
00:06:30.940 --> 00:06:35.360
and each user's transitive group memberships,
which need User dot Read dot All.

102
00:06:35.360 --> 00:06:39.410
Those are what the scan and the residual
review read from a real tenant.

103
00:06:39.410 --> 00:06:44.498
Step two lints the program's three policy
files over the fixture population.

104
00:06:44.498 --> 00:06:48.648
Zero findings, zero warnings,
zero information items.

105
00:06:48.648 --> 00:06:52.589
That clean run is part of
the evidence an ISSO keeps,

106
00:06:52.589 --> 00:06:55.490
beside the hash of each body sent.

107
00:06:59.240 --> 00:07:01.872
Step three lints five broken policies:

108
00:07:01.872 --> 00:07:05.490
five findings and two warnings,
and an exit code of two.

109
00:07:05.490 --> 00:07:07.790
Find the three this module cares about.

110
00:07:07.790 --> 00:07:13.115
A A oh one: a policy whose rule uses
member-of, which will be quarantined.

111
00:07:13.115 --> 00:07:16.659
A A oh two: two automatic
policies on one package,

112
00:07:16.659 --> 00:07:21.565
and two users in the population
match both, so they can lose access.

113
00:07:21.565 --> 00:07:26.106
And A A oh nine: tier one administrator
eligibility assigned by rule,

114
00:07:26.106 --> 00:07:30.890
when privileged access should be
requested and activated through PIM.

115
00:07:30.890 --> 00:07:32.903
The slide trims the other four.

116
00:07:32.903 --> 00:07:35.827
Step four turns from
the files to the tenant.

117
00:07:35.827 --> 00:07:40.333
The member-of scan reads an export
of the tenant's assignment policies,

118
00:07:40.333 --> 00:07:44.916
four of them, and one still uses
member-of: a legacy shared drives package.

119
00:07:44.916 --> 00:07:46.844
That's the M S oh one finding,

120
00:07:46.844 --> 00:07:50.970
and the evidence you want is
this scan with zero rows.

121
00:07:54.720 --> 00:07:57.345
Step five lists one mover's residual.

122
00:07:57.345 --> 00:08:01.858
You give it the mover, the mover's
assignments and group memberships, the

123
00:08:01.858 --> 00:08:06.907
program's policies, a moment, the first of
November, the cloud and an output folder.

124
00:08:06.907 --> 00:08:11.108
The mover holds eight items, and
three go to the residual review.

125
00:08:11.108 --> 00:08:16.051
Print the review sheet: one requested
package, the grants reporting workspace,

126
00:08:16.051 --> 00:08:18.408
and two groups held outside packages,

127
00:08:18.408 --> 00:08:22.058
a legacy timekeeping approvers
group and a finance share.

128
00:08:22.058 --> 00:08:24.019
Those three are the warnings.

129
00:08:24.019 --> 00:08:28.216
The finding is R V oh two: the
move took effect on October 19,

130
00:08:28.216 --> 00:08:33.395
and the review is still open thirteen
days later, past the program's ten.

131
00:08:33.395 --> 00:08:35.816
That sheet goes to the manager of record,

132
00:08:35.816 --> 00:08:39.557
and the manager's decisions and
the interval are evidence too.

133
00:08:39.557 --> 00:08:45.250
Step six runs the tests, fifty-seven
pass, and you delete the chapter's output.

134
00:08:49.000 --> 00:08:54.626
Lab 2 runs the gate in your own test tenant:
worldwide or G C C, never production,

135
00:08:54.626 --> 00:08:58.197
with Entra ID Governance
trial or test licenses,

136
00:08:58.197 --> 00:09:03.287
two test access packages, and three
test users with coded attributes.

137
00:09:03.287 --> 00:09:06.461
Export the tenant's policies,
and run the scan beside

138
00:09:06.461 --> 00:09:09.338
Learn's read-only script,
so you can compare them.

139
00:09:09.338 --> 00:09:11.350
Create one automatic policy.

140
00:09:11.350 --> 00:09:16.600
Move a test user through the pipeline,
never by hand, and run the residual review.

141
00:09:16.600 --> 00:09:19.763
The reader app needs a
certificate and two permissions,

142
00:09:19.763 --> 00:09:23.475
EntitlementManagement dot Read
dot All and User dot Read dot All.

143
00:09:23.475 --> 00:09:27.500
Creating the policy takes the Identity
Governance Administrator role.

144
00:09:27.500 --> 00:09:29.732
To back it out, remove the test policy,

145
00:09:29.732 --> 00:09:32.633
choosing whether its
assignments are removed,

146
00:09:32.633 --> 00:09:37.022
restore the test user's code through
the pipeline, remove the reader app,

147
00:09:37.022 --> 00:09:41.113
its permissions and certificate,
and delete the export and output.

148
00:09:41.113 --> 00:09:43.630
The authors haven't run Lab 2.

149
00:09:47.380 --> 00:09:50.255
Two things differ by where
you are and what you grant.

150
00:09:50.255 --> 00:09:52.130
First, government clouds.

151
00:09:52.130 --> 00:09:57.038
If your agency is in GCC High, note the
gate's availability gap: administrative

152
00:09:57.038 --> 00:10:01.792
units with dynamic membership rules
aren't available in Azure Government.

153
00:10:01.792 --> 00:10:05.393
The guide's Step 4 gives the
gap and its interim path.

154
00:10:05.393 --> 00:10:08.542
Second, privileged
access, wherever you are.

155
00:10:08.542 --> 00:10:12.373
A package that grants tier one
administrator eligibility to

156
00:10:12.373 --> 00:10:16.579
everyone whose organization code
matches is the A A oh nine finding.

157
00:10:16.579 --> 00:10:20.340
HR attributes should decide
who may request eligibility,

158
00:10:20.340 --> 00:10:22.768
and the role is activated through PIM.

159
00:10:22.768 --> 00:10:25.330
That's what the program's
request policy does.

160
00:10:25.330 --> 00:10:30.393
CISA's scuba policy seven point five
keeps highly privileged roles only in

161
00:10:30.393 --> 00:10:36.370
a privileged access management system,
and under BOD 25-01 it's required.

162
00:10:40.120 --> 00:10:41.507
The federal requirement.

163
00:10:41.507 --> 00:10:46.235
This gate meets NIST special publication
eight hundred fifty-three A C two,

164
00:10:46.235 --> 00:10:48.598
which asks you to authorize access on

165
00:10:48.598 --> 00:10:52.258
organization-defined attributes
and to review accounts.

166
00:10:52.258 --> 00:10:57.933
It meets A C six, least privilege,
and A C five, separation of duties.

167
00:10:57.933 --> 00:11:03.333
It meets P S five, personnel transfer,
which is what the residual review is for.

168
00:11:03.333 --> 00:11:08.141
And it meets scuba policy seven point
five, highly privileged roles only

169
00:11:08.141 --> 00:11:13.832
through a privileged access management
system, required under BOD 25-01.

170
00:11:13.832 --> 00:11:18.856
The evidence an ISSO keeps for
Gate 4: the reviewed policy files,

171
00:11:18.856 --> 00:11:22.720
the lint's clean run, and
the hash of each body sent.

172
00:11:22.720 --> 00:11:25.520
The member-of scan's
output with zero rows.

173
00:11:25.520 --> 00:11:27.308
A A oh nine's clean run.

174
00:11:27.308 --> 00:11:29.056
Each mover's residual sheet,

175
00:11:29.056 --> 00:11:32.857
the manager's decisions and the
transfer-to-review interval.

176
00:11:32.857 --> 00:11:37.080
And the separation-of-duties settings
in the package configuration.

177
00:11:40.830 --> 00:11:42.805
Check yourself with two questions.

178
00:11:42.805 --> 00:11:44.962
First: from November 3, 2026,

179
00:11:44.962 --> 00:11:50.942
what happens to an automatic assignment
policy whose rule uses member-of?

180
00:11:50.942 --> 00:11:53.417
The answer is b: it's quarantined.

181
00:11:53.417 --> 00:11:57.880
It stays, but it adds and removes
nothing until the operator is removed,

182
00:11:57.880 --> 00:12:01.804
and Learn publishes a read-only
script to find those policies.

183
00:12:01.804 --> 00:12:06.417
Second: in the residual review, which
items go to the manager of record?

184
00:12:06.417 --> 00:12:10.343
The answer is c: assignments
that came from request policies,

185
00:12:10.343 --> 00:12:12.542
and groups held outside packages.

186
00:12:12.542 --> 00:12:16.335
Everything a rule manages,
kept, removed after grace,

187
00:12:16.335 --> 00:12:20.217
package-managed or rule-managed,
takes care of itself.

188
00:12:20.217 --> 00:12:25.480
The module page has two more questions,
each linked to the section that answers it.

189
00:12:29.230 --> 00:12:30.055
To sum up.

190
00:12:30.055 --> 00:12:34.980
You linted three good and five broken
assignment policies over a population.

191
00:12:34.980 --> 00:12:37.918
You found a member-of
policy in a tenant export.

192
00:12:37.918 --> 00:12:42.493
And you listed one mover's residual
access, with a review that's overdue.

193
00:12:42.493 --> 00:12:47.195
Carry one thing forward: a move reaches
the directory through the pipeline,

194
00:12:47.195 --> 00:12:51.367
never by hand, and then the rules
and the residual review do the rest.

195
00:12:51.367 --> 00:12:55.506
Next is Module 5: check
certificate-based authentication.

196
00:12:55.506 --> 00:12:59.124
You'll check the certificate-based
authentication trust store,

197
00:12:59.124 --> 00:13:02.950
and each certificate revocation
list, against your cloud's limits.
